Task 1: Understanding Apache Camel as an Integration Framework
Subtask 1.1: Introduction to Apache Camel
Apache Camel is an open-source integration framework that implements Enterprise Integration Patterns (EIPs) to facilitate communication between different systems.

Key Features:

Supports multiple protocols (HTTP, JMS, FTP, etc.).
Provides domain-specific languages (DSL) like Java, XML, and YAML.
Lightweight and embeddable in Java applications.
Subtask 1.2: Camel’s Core Components
Camel consists of:

Routes – Define the flow of messages.
Components – Connectors to external systems (e.g., camel-http, camel-jms).
Processors – Modify or route messages (e.g., filter, transform).
Expected Outcome:

Understand Camel’s purpose and architecture.
Task 2: Understanding Enterprise Integration Patterns (EIPs)
Subtask 2.1: Common EIPs in Camel
Camel implements EIPs such as:

Message Routing (Content-Based Router, Recipient List).
Message Transformation (Enricher, Content Enricher).
Messaging Systems (Pipes and Filters, Message Bus).
Example:
A Content-Based Router directs messages based on their content:

from("direct:start")  
    .choice()  
        .when(header("type").isEqualTo("A"))  
            .to("direct:routeA")  
        .when(header("type").isEqualTo("B"))  
            .to("direct:routeB")  
    .otherwise()  
        .to("direct:default");  
Subtask 2.2: EIP Use Cases
Aggregator – Combine multiple messages into one.
Splitter – Break a message into smaller parts.
Expected Outcome:

Recognize key EIPs and their implementation in Camel.
Task 3: Creating a Simple Camel Route Using Java DSL
Subtask 3.1: Setting Up a Maven Project
Generate a new Maven project:
mvn archetype:generate -DgroupId=com.example -DartifactId=camel-lab -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart -DinteractiveMode=false
Add Camel dependencies in pom.xml:
<dependencies>  
    <dependency>  
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>  
        <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>  
        <version>3.20.0</version>  
    </dependency>  
    <dependency>  
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>  
        <artifactId>camel-main</artifactId>  
        <version>3.20.0</version>  
    </dependency>  
</dependencies>  
Subtask 3.2: Writing a Basic Camel Route
Create a Java class SimpleRouteBuilder.java:
import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;  

public class SimpleRouteBuilder extends RouteBuilder {  
    @Override  
    public void configure() throws Exception {  
        from("timer:myTimer?period=1000")  
            .log("Hello from Apache Camel!");  
    }  
}  
Create a Main class to run the route:
import org.apache.camel.main.Main;  

public class CamelApp {  
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {  
        Main main = new Main();  
        main.configure().addRoutesBuilder(new SimpleRouteBuilder());  
        main.run();  
    }  
}  
Subtask 3.3: Running the Application
Compile and run:
mvn compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="CamelApp"
Expected Output:
[imer://myTimer] Hello from Apache Camel!  
[imer://myTimer] Hello from Apache Camel!  
Troubleshooting Tips:

If the app doesn’t run, check Maven dependencies (mvn clean install).
Ensure Main class is correctly configured.
Expected Outcome:

A running Camel application logging messages every second.
